      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "STUDY OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To assess the correlation in an older pediatric population between patient self-reported visual analog scale (VAS) pain scores and observational pain-related behavior scores."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "DESIGN",
          "content": "Prospective, comparative study."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "SETTING",
          "content": "Inpatient surgical units of a free-standing children's hospital."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "PATIENTS",
          "content": "30 ASA physical status I and II outpatients, 8 to 16 years of age, undergoing a variety of orthopedic, plastic, urologic, and general surgical procedures."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "INTERVENTIONS",
          "content": "Each patient underwent a single assessment of pain intensity on the first postoperative day."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "MEASUREMENTS AND MAIN RESULTS",
          "content": "Three health care providers (a clinical nurse specialist, a registered nurse with extensive pediatric experience, and a child life specialist) simultaneously generated an independent pain-related behavioral score (range of 0 = no pain to 100 = worst pain possible) based on their subjective perceptions of the patient's observed facial expression, activity level, and breathing pattern. The patient was then asked to provide a self-reported VAS pain score (range of 0 = \"no pain\" with a smiling face, to 100 = \"worst pain ever\" with a frowning face). A single set of such concurrent pain scores was obtained once from each study patient. The pain-related behavior scores displayed a satisfactory interrater reliability, with an observed interclass correlation coefficient (Cohen's kappa value) of 0.83. When compared with a patient's self-reported VAS score, the three pain related behavioral scores generated by each health care provider for a given patient exhibited both variable and minimal correlation."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "A tenuous relationship may exist between on older child's own perception of pain intensity and his or her behavioral expression of that pain as interpreted by a health care provider."
        }
      ]},
